## 3. Methodology & Execution
*Document the core steps you took to complete the objective. Focus on the **why**, not just the **what**. Do not dump every single command you typed—capture the important decisions.*
1. **Initial Assessment:** Examining the skew, glare and obstructions
   * *Observation:* The target image was taken at an angle, and the ID was not even on either axis, had a mild glare, and was slightly covered by an envelope.
2. **Configuration / Execution:** Beginning with the skew, then moving onto the glare and covered corner of the ID, then finally moving onto changing the ID name and photo.
   * *Reasoning:* Starting with removing skew in PhotoCraft gave the image an inital crop to remove the unneeded parts of the image and set it up for the next steps. next used Gemini to remove glare and repair the covered portion of the ID. Then finally matched the font using a font-identification site and replaced the photo on the ID with a photo of myself.
3. **Validation:** Compared the ID badge I created to the mock employee's badge in class after it was printed.
## 4. Challenges & Troubleshooting
*No IT project goes perfectly. What broke, and how did you fix it? (Hiring managers specifically look for this section to see your problem-solving skills).*
* **Issue Encountered:** AI hallucinating things it wasn't asked to onto the image.
* **Troubleshooting Steps:** Reprompting, and reverting from a save.
* **Resolution:** Reprompted the AI to fix the hallucinated parts, and if that didn't work I revereted from a save file and prompted it in a different way to get the desired effect.
## 5. Conclusion & Lessons Learned
*Summarize the final outcome and the professional takeaways.*
**Final Outcome:** ![Modified ID Badge](RedTeam Badge In-Class.jpg) 
*Caption: Edited ID Badge
* **Lessons Learned:** Always verify an ID badge beyond physical inspection because it is incredibly easy and expeditious to manufacture a fake ID badge off of a simple photo.
* **Security Recommendations:** If applicable, ensure every employee utilizes the physical hardware embedded in access cards, don't rely on a quick physical inspection, and don't allow employee's to piggyback entry using a different employee's ID badge.

